<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-leagues">
  <title>Watching soccer---the English leagues</title>

  <epigraph>
    <q>Football: it's a funny old game.</q>
    <attribution>Jimmy Greaves</attribution>
  </epigraph>

  <introduction>
    <p><em>Background.</em> The English soccer league is the oldest in the world, the first season being 1888-89.</p>
    <p><em>Questions.</em> Are there more draws now than before? Has home advantage always been the same? Do teams score fewer goals now? Can we display team histories over years? How do teams perform over a season?</p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> The <em>engsoccerdata</em> package contains results for all English soccer games in the top four tiers from 1888-89 to 2021-22 (@curley:2022).</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> Results of games summarised in 12 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="secFootball1">
    <title>History of the English football leagues since 1888</title>

    <p>The English league started with one division of 12 teams in 1888 and quickly added another in 1892. After World War I a third division started and then split North/South. In 1958 the two thirds were merged and split into national third and fourth tiers. In 1992 the old first division became the Premier League and other divisions were renamed too. For simplicity the four levels are called tiers.</p>

    <p>Much historical detail is available on Wikipedia season pages. The package maintainer has also published insightful analyses (@curley:2016).</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secFootball2">
    <title>How have numbers of draws changed?</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socD">
      <caption>Rate of draws over the years in the English league's top tier with a gam smooth.</caption>
      <image source="images/socD-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The proportion of drawn games in the top tier climbed until World War I, stayed fairly constant for decades, rose again in the 1960s, and dipped slightly after 3 points for a win was introduced in 1981. Seasonal variability remained substantial.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socDf">
      <caption>Smooths of the rates of draws for the top tiers in Italy (dark blue), England (light blue), and Spain (red).</caption>
      <image source="images/socDf-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Rates in Italy and Spain differed markedly. Italy was higher in the 1970s and 1980s, while Spain was lower, then briefly higher than England, then lower again. In the most recent years shown, Spain has the highest draw rate of the three.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secFootball3">
    <title>How big is home advantage?</title>

    <p>Home advantage is a common feature of team sports (@gomez:2022). One measure is the proportion of points won at home. Figure <xref ref="fig-socHA"/> uses a consistent 3-points-for-a-win convention across all years.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socHA">
      <caption>Proportions of points won at home over time in the various tiers of the English league.</caption>
      <image source="images/socHA-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The lower three tiers show relatively steady decline since about 1980. In tier 1 there is a dramatic drop below 50% in 2020-21 when most games were played without fans due to Covid.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socHAt">
      <caption>Proportions of points won at home for every team for every season.</caption>
      <image source="images/socHAt-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Team-level points show striking outliers, including seasons with no away points and rare strongly negative home proportions. Several such teams were relegated.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secFootball4">
    <title>Have the numbers of goals scored gone down?</title>

    <p>Goals per game were very high in the first years, then declined quickly. The 1925 offside-law change caused a short-term surge. After a post-war rise peaking around 1960, scoring declined until about 1970 and then remained fairly stable near 2.5 goals per game.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socG">
      <caption>The numbers of goals per game for the four tiers of the English league (the change in the offside law is marked with a red dashed line).</caption>
      <image source="images/socG-1.png"/>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secFootball5">
    <title>How well have teams performed over the years?</title>

    <p>League tables from all divisions can be combined into an overall seasonal ranking. Special handling is needed for periods with split third divisions and for the disrupted 2019-20 lower-tier season.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-teamBPS">
      <caption>Comparing the three South Coast teams: Bournemouth, Portsmouth, and Southampton.</caption>
      <image source="images/teamBPS-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Portsmouth were strongest for a long period. Southampton were mostly strongest from the 1960s and briefly dropped to tier 3 before rebounding. Bournemouth were generally below the other two until recent years.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-teamBPSq">
      <caption>Comparing Bournemouth, Portsmouth, and Southampton on a square root scale.</caption>
      <image source="images/teamBPSq-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Using square-root transformed ranks emphasizes top-end differences more than linear ranks.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secFootball6">
    <title>Wormcharts show league progress over a season</title>

    <p>Wormcharts show how team positions evolve over a season by plotting cumulative points relative to the round-average. The 2015-16 chart shows Leicester's surprising title run and clear separation at top and bottom.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-worm15">
      <caption>Team performances in points above/below the average in 2015-16.</caption>
      <image source="images/worm15-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The 1982-83 chart highlights Liverpool's strong season and also notable late-season collapses and recoveries among relegation contenders.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-worm82">
      <caption>Team performances in points above/below the average in season 1982-83. There were 22 teams and each played 42 games.</caption>
      <image source="images/worm82-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Comparing the most equal and least equal seasons among 22-team, 2-points-for-win championships shows very different spread patterns.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-worm3768">
      <caption>Wormcharts for the two seasons with extreme point ranges for the English 1st Division Championship with 22 teams and two points for a win between 1919-20 and 1980-81.</caption>
      <image source="images/worm3768-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p><em>Answers.</em> Draw rates increased from very low initial values but have changed little on average in the last 40 years. Home advantage declined, especially in lower tiers. Goals per game have been fairly steady over the last 50 years. Wormcharts give clear seasonal progression views.</p>
    <p><em>Further questions.</em> Would goal difference instead of goal average before 1976 have changed outcomes? Which teams spent longest in each tier? Which moved between tiers most often?</p>

    <p><em>Graphical takeaways.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Time-series graphics should mark rule or context changes, with guidelines where helpful. (Figures <xref ref="fig-socD"/>, <xref ref="fig-socDf"/>, and <xref ref="fig-socG"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Drawing cases of interest bigger, in colour, and on top is effective highlighting. (Figure <xref ref="fig-socHAt"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Transformations can emphasize different parts of a scale. (Figure <xref ref="fig-teamBPSq"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Wormcharts illuminate how a league progresses over a season. (Figures <xref ref="fig-worm15"/> to <xref ref="fig-worm3768"/>)</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</chapter>
